<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
    The complete schema for the Iris proactive struggle intervention (#350), in one migration.

    Additive and non-destructive: four nullable columns plus an index on the existing iris_message table, and one
    new table. No drops, no alters of existing columns, no data backfill. Existing iris_message rows get NULL.

    iris_message gains:
      origin                 marks a row as proactive, so it can be excluded from the AI rate limit, included in
                             the chat overview, and rendered differently in the history sent to Pyris
      proactive_outcome      how the episode ended; the first terminal value wins and is never overwritten
      proactive_episode_id   ties the rows of one episode together for the episode-scoped outcome endpoint
      proactive_exercise_id  the exercise the row was decided for, so a client-reused episode id cannot make one
                             exercise's outcome terminal for another

    iris_proactive_episode registers each episode when its trigger is accepted, so the episode has a row that can be
    locked and a place to hold its terminal outcome even before its first message exists. The same row carries the
    episode's ambient offer, because the offer has exactly the same natural key. Details on the changeSet.

    episode_id is NOT NULL and its width matches iris_message.proactive_episode_id, so an id that can be registered
    can always be persisted on the message too.

    Three changeSets, split only where the databases genuinely differ: origin and proactive_outcome are a native
    ENUM on MySQL/H2 and text on PostgreSQL, which is how the rest of this directory handles an enum column. The
    dbms attribute is routing, not a guard.

    No preConditions. This changelog is unreleased and has been consolidated in place: earlier revisions carried a
    separate iris_ambient_decision table, whose columns now live on iris_proactive_episode, and the changeSets were
    rewritten rather than superseded. A database that ran an earlier revision must therefore be recreated - its
    recorded checksums no longer match. Given that, a per-piece onFail="MARK_RAN" guard would only defend a
    partially migrated database, which is exactly the state this file already declares unsupported, and on a
    compound changeSet it would be actively harmful: one satisfied precondition would mark the whole changeSet as
    ran and silently leave the rest of the schema missing.
    -->

    <!--
    Every episode lookup filters on (proactive_episode_id, proactive_exercise_id), so the composite index carries
    the two leading predicates; one query additionally filters proactive_outcome, which the index does not cover.

    proactive_exercise_id exists because proactive_episode_id alone does not identify an episode: the id is
    client-generated, so the same id can be sent for two different exercises. Scoped only by the owning user, which
    closes the IDOR, one exercise's terminal outcome then marked the other exercise's episode as finished.

    The exercise cannot be derived from the session. iris_session.entity_id and chat_mode change on every context
    switch (a session is born a COURSE_CHAT and is switched to an exercise later), so the session records where the
    chat points NOW, not which exercise a historical row belongs to. The binding therefore lives on the message row,
    written once at insert time and never updated.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20260827160638-1-add-proactive-columns-mysql" author="liam-berger" dbms="mysql,h2">
        <addColumn tableName="iris_message">
            <column name="origin" type="ENUM('PROACTIVE_STRUGGLE')"/>
            <column name="proactive_outcome" type="ENUM('DISMISSED','RECOVERED','ABANDONED','INTERRUPTED')"/>
            <column name="proactive_episode_id" type="varchar(64)"/>
            <column name="proactive_exercise_id" type="BIGINT"/>
        </addColumn>
        <createIndex tableName="iris_message" indexName="idx_iris_message_proactive_episode_exercise">
            <column name="proactive_episode_id"/>
            <column name="proactive_exercise_id"/>
        </createIndex>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260827160638-1-add-proactive-columns-postgres" author="liam-berger" dbms="postgresql">
        <addColumn tableName="iris_message">
            <column name="origin" type="text"/>
            <column name="proactive_outcome" type="text"/>
            <column name="proactive_episode_id" type="varchar(64)"/>
            <column name="proactive_exercise_id" type="BIGINT"/>
        </addColumn>
        <createIndex tableName="iris_message" indexName="idx_iris_message_proactive_episode_exercise">
            <column name="proactive_episode_id"/>
            <column name="proactive_exercise_id"/>
        </createIndex>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
    The episode as a row of its own, so it has an identity that can be locked.

    Every terminal decision used to be a check-then-act: a callback read whether the episode was already closed and
    wrote its message in a separate statement, so a dismiss committing in between produced a hint the student had
    already dismissed. There was nothing to serialize on. The chat session cannot serve as the mutex, because a
    session is resolved as "the latest for (user, exercise)" with no uniqueness constraint, so an episode's existing
    message and its next append can sit in different sessions and take different locks. The episode could not lock
    itself either, since before its first message row it had no row at all.

    A row is written when Artemis accepts a trigger, which is also what makes an outcome arriving before the first
    message representable instead of deferred. outcome is the authoritative terminal state;
    iris_message.proactive_outcome stays as a subordinate mirror for the history replayed to Pyris.

    The row also carries the episode's ambient offer. The pull model tells the client "there is a hint for this
    episode" without persisting a chat message, and writes the message only when the student clicks reveal, so the
    server has to keep its own copy of what it offered: reveal persists hint_text, never the caller's text, which is
    what stops a student from authoring assistant history that is fed back into the Pyris prompt. consumed_at and
    consumed_message_id make an offer single-use and a replayed reveal idempotent. Those three columns lived in a
    table of their own until this changelog was consolidated. It was keyed on the same (user, exercise, episode)
    triple, so it had the same grain, and keeping it apart cost a second unique key, a second lock, and an insert
    race that existed only because the offer had to create its own row. They are nullable because a registered
    episode legitimately has no offer yet.

    NOT NULL on every column that takes part in the unique key, so no nullable column takes part in the uniqueness.
    last_triggered_at is indexed for retention: every trigger refreshes it, so the cleanup asks "no trigger for
    seven days" rather than "registered seven days ago" and never reaps an episode out from under a run in flight.
    The foreign keys cascade so an episode never outlives its user or its exercise; a deleted course cascades
    through its exercises.

    idx_iris_proactive_episode_exercise is created BEFORE the exercise foreign key, so InnoDB adopts it as the
    key's supporting index instead of silently creating a second one of its own. PostgreSQL creates no index for a
    foreign key at all, and without one the ON DELETE CASCADE from exercise (and from a course cascading through
    its exercises) scans this table in full. The unique key already covers the user_id reference, because user_id
    is its leading column.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20260827160638-2-create-iris-proactive-episode" author="liam-berger">
        <createTable tableName="iris_proactive_episode">
            <column name="id" type="BIGINT" autoIncrement="true">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="user_id" type="BIGINT">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="exercise_id" type="BIGINT">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="episode_id" type="varchar(64)">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="outcome" type="varchar(32)"/>
            <column name="last_triggered_at" type="datetime(3)">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="hint_text" type="text"/>
            <column name="consumed_at" type="datetime(3)"/>
            <column name="consumed_message_id" type="BIGINT"/>
        </createTable>
        <addUniqueConstraint tableName="iris_proactive_episode" columnNames="user_id, exercise_id, episode_id"
                             constraintName="ux_iris_proactive_episode_user_exercise_episode"/>
        <createIndex tableName="iris_proactive_episode" indexName="idx_iris_proactive_episode_last_triggered_at">
            <column name="last_triggered_at"/>
        </createIndex>
        <createIndex tableName="iris_proactive_episode" indexName="idx_iris_proactive_episode_exercise">
            <column name="exercise_id"/>
        </createIndex>
        <addForeignKeyConstraint baseColumnNames="user_id" baseTableName="iris_proactive_episode" constraintName="fk_iris_proactive_episode_user_id"
                                 referencedColumnNames="id" referencedTableName="jhi_user" onDelete="CASCADE" onUpdate="RESTRICT"/>
        <addForeignKeyConstraint baseColumnNames="exercise_id" baseTableName="iris_proactive_episode" constraintName="fk_iris_proactive_episode_exercise_id"
                                 referencedColumnNames="id" referencedTableName="exercise" onDelete="CASCADE" onUpdate="RESTRICT"/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
